WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.081
Do not buy

00:00:00.892 --> 00:00:02.295
RAM compress

00:00:02.814 --> 00:00:03.839
A post argues

00:00:03.651 --> 00:00:04.913
that compressing RAM

00:00:04.795 --> 00:00:05.502
is a better

00:00:05.314 --> 00:00:06.422
answer than buying

00:00:06.234 --> 00:00:07.071
more memory

00:00:07.295 --> 00:00:08.863
explaining how compression

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:09.901
can stretch available

00:00:09.713 --> 00:00:10.361
memory

00:00:10.894 --> 00:00:11.943
Recently I was

00:00:11.755 --> 00:00:12.462
running out of

00:00:12.274 --> 00:00:12.981
memory

00:00:13.135 --> 00:00:13.912
I found my

00:00:13.724 --> 00:00:15.021
solution in zram

00:00:15.257 --> 00:00:16.200
a solution to

00:00:16.012 --> 00:00:17.273
compress memory

00:00:17.412 --> 00:00:18.638
Often the CPUs

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:19.358
were under little

00:00:19.169 --> 00:00:20.313
stress so we

00:00:20.125 --> 00:00:20.961
could have avoided

00:00:20.773 --> 00:00:21.810
upgrading if we

00:00:21.622 --> 00:00:22.671
had enabled RAM

00:00:22.483 --> 00:00:23.320
compression

00:00:23.846 --> 00:00:25.320
Unfortunately not all

00:00:25.131 --> 00:00:26.204
Linux environments

00:00:26.028 --> 00:00:27.395
have RAM compression

00:00:27.219 --> 00:00:28.610
enabled by default

00:00:28.749 --> 00:00:29.904
Compressing memory

00:00:29.716 --> 00:00:30.482
does come with

00:00:30.294 --> 00:00:30.965
small

00:00:31.025 --> 00:00:31.944
but not really

00:00:31.756 --> 00:00:32.593
noticeable

00:00:33.126 --> 00:00:34.140
Full story on

00:00:33.951 --> 00:00:34.847
the dashboard
